abstract: 'This paper establishes a universal saturation density scale, rho_T ~ 20
  g/cm^3 (a saturation scale of the temporal-field topology, not an ambient-density
  switch), that organizes gravitational anomalies across 18 orders of magnitude in
  mass. Terrestrial calibration from GNSS atomic clock coherence limits yields a characteristic
  length L_c ~ 4200 km for Earth. This density is externally calibrated from GNSS
  atomic clock measurements. The SPARC galaxy database confirms the predicted R_DM
  ∝ M^(1/3) scaling law (alpha = 0.355 ± 0.043 (bootstrap, 1000 resamples)). A Temporal Topology screening analysis
  of 26 objects (S ∝ rho^(1/3)) explains why precision GR tests pass while galactic
  dynamics show deviations. The externally calibrated value enables tightly constrained
  astrophysical applications, including testing the soliton hypothesis on RBH-1 (Paper 7).

  '
